---
import "../styles/global.css";

const basePath = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/?$/, "/");
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width" />
    <meta name="description" content="How Foxhole Forecast settles and scores timed model predictions." />
    <title>Scoring Method · Foxhole Forecast</title>
  </head>
  <body>
    <header class="method-masthead">
      <div class="masthead__eyebrow">HOW WE CHECK THE CALLS · A PLAIN-LANGUAGE GUIDE</div>
      <div class="method-hero page-width">
        <div>
          <p class="kicker">Methodology</p>
          <h1>How we check<br /><span>the calls</span></h1>
          <p class="sublede">Every prediction names a base, what should happen to it, when, and how sure the model is. Later observations from the public War API tell us how well that call held up.</p>
        </div>
        <a class="method-back" href={basePath}>← Return to live scores</a>
      </div>
    </header>

    <nav class="section-nav" aria-label="Scoring sections">
      <div class="page-width">
        <a href="#forecast">The question</a>
        <a href="#settlement">What happened?</a>
        <a href="#timing">Timing</a>
        <a href="#outcome">Partial credit</a>
        <a href="#aggregation">Leaderboard</a>
        <a href="#selection">Where to look</a>
        <a href="#censoring">Fair scoring</a>
        <a href="#limitations">Limits</a>
        <a href="#rationale">Rationale</a>
      </div>
    </nav>

    <main class="scoring-page page-width">
      <section class="method-intro">
        <p class="kicker">The short version</p>
        <p>We ask each model to make eight specific, timed calls. Then we compare those calls with the next observed ownership changes. A model earns trust by being right about <em>where</em>, <em>what</em>, and <em>when</em>—not by writing a convincing briefing.</p>
        <aside class="method-callout"><strong>Headline score</strong><span>The 0–100 score is a compact summary of timing and outcome quality. Higher is better; it is not a percentage of predictions that were correct.</span></aside>
      </section>

      <section class="method-limitations" id="limitations">
        <p class="kicker">Before reading the ranking</p>
        <h2>This is a small experiment with a deliberately narrow view.</h2>
        <div class="method-limitations__grid">
          <p>We use only the public Foxhole War API and try to minimize out-of-pocket model costs. That makes the comparison repeatable: models see the same cutoff-safe information.</p>
          <p>Knowing a base's artillery-shell stockpile and how quickly shells are used or resupplied could be more directly predictive than a region-wide casualty count. The public API does not expose those logistics, and we do not scrape game state or operate an in-game computer-use agent to obtain them.</p>
          <p>So a score is evidence about this setup, not a claim that a model understands the whole war or will always be a good battlefield adviser.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="forecast" class="scoring-section">
        <div class="scoring-section__heading"><p class="kicker">01</p><h2>What a model must forecast</h2></div>
        <div class="rule-grid">
          <article class="rule-card"><strong>8</strong><h3>Different bases</h3><p>Every round contains eight ranked predictions about eight different strategic bases.</p></article>
          <article class="rule-card"><strong>4 + 4</strong><h3>Two time horizons</h3><p>Four calls cover the next six hours; four cover the following 6–24 hours. The model must name a specific time for each.</p></article>
          <article class="rule-card"><strong>2</strong><h3>Possible outcomes</h3><p><b>Captured</b> means ownership changes to the opposing faction. <b>Destroyed</b> means a faction-owned base becomes neutral.</p></article>
          <article class="rule-card"><strong>1–5</strong><h3>Cited clues</h3><p>Each call cites one to five cutoff-safe inputs. The model’s 1–10 relevance ratings stay visible for auditability; they are not an objective truth score.</p></article>
        </div>
        <p class="method-note">Each prediction includes confidence from 0.00 to 1.00—the chance that the named outcome happens by the deadline—and a timing-width estimate. A same-faction capture call is dropped as a model error. Rank records priority but does not change weight. <span class="technical-label">Technical fields: <code>confidence</code>, <code>sigma_minutes</code>.</span></p>
      </section>

      <section id="settlement" class="scoring-section">
        <div class="scoring-section__heading"><p class="kicker">02</p><h2>How a prediction resolves</h2></div>
        <div class="rule-grid rule-grid--two">
          <article class="rule-card"><h3>Give each call time</h3><p>Each prediction stays open until three hours after its named ETA. We look for the first ownership transition after the model’s cutoff and before that deadline.</p></article>
          <article class="rule-card"><h3>Respect Foxhole’s sequence</h3><p>A base may become neutral before an enemy rebuild completes. A named enemy capture gets full outcome credit; destruction before that capture gets partial credit. Invalid same-faction calls are dropped and explained on the round page.</p></article>
        </div>
        <p class="method-note">If no transition occurs by the deadline and coverage is adequate, the call is a scored miss. The model is penalized in proportion to the chance it assigned to an event that did not happen.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="timing" class="scoring-section">
        <div class="scoring-section__heading"><p class="kicker">03</p><h2>The event-time distribution</h2></div>
        <p>A model can be early or late even when it identifies the right base. We therefore score not only whether the event happened, but how close the predicted time was. A narrow timing window earns more when it is well placed and loses more when it is overconfident.</p>
        <p class="method-caption technical-label">Technical detail: the conditional event time uses a truncated Normal distribution centered on the ETA, with <code>sigma_minutes</code> as its standard deviation. Confidence supplies the total probability mass.</p>
        <div class="formula-block">
          <span>forecast curve</span>
          <strong>F(t) = confidence × truncated Normal CDF(t; ETA, sigma)</strong>
          <span>CRPS</span>
          <strong>integral from cutoff to ETA + 3h of (F(t) − observed cumulative event(t))² dt</strong>
        </div>
        <p class="method-caption">The API gives an interval rather than an exact event second. We average the observed event time uniformly across that interval, so the score respects the collection cadence without pretending to know more than was observed.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="outcome" class="scoring-section">
        <div class="scoring-section__heading"><p class="kicker">04</p><h2>When outcomes are close</h2></div>
        <div class="formula-block formula-block--compact"><span>observed curve height</span><strong>1.00 for the named outcome · 0.75 for capture/destruction confusion · 0.00 for no qualifying outcome</strong></div>
        <div class="outcome-table" role="table" aria-label="Outcome credit table">
          <div class="outcome-table__row outcome-table__head" role="row"><span>Prediction vs. observation</span><span>Outcome credit</span></div>
          <div class="outcome-table__row" role="row"><span>Captured by named faction / Named faction capture</span><strong class="score-value">1.00</strong></div>
          <div class="outcome-table__row" role="row"><span>Destroyed / Destroyed</span><strong class="score-value">1.00</strong></div>
          <div class="outcome-table__row" role="row"><span>Faction capture / Destroyed, or Destroyed / Faction capture</span><strong class="score-value">0.75</strong></div>
          <div class="outcome-table__row" role="row"><span>No qualifying transition by the deadline</span><strong class="score-value score-value--miss">0.00</strong></div>
        </div>
        <div class="worked-example">
          <p class="kicker">Worked example</p>
          <h3>Correct capture, but later than expected</h3>
          <p>If a model predicts a capture at 10:00 and the API observes the relevant transition around 11:00, it receives credit for identifying the event but pays a timing penalty. The technical score measures the gap between those two time curves.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="aggregation" class="scoring-section">
        <div class="scoring-section__heading"><p class="kicker">05</p><h2>Putting the evidence together</h2></div>
        <div class="rule-grid rule-grid--two">
          <article class="rule-card"><h3>Whole forecast error <span class="technical-label">(CRPS)</span></h3><p>This is the average distance between what the model said and what the API observed, in minutes. Lower is better. We keep near-term and next-day calls separate so one style does not hide the other.</p></article>
          <article class="rule-card"><h3>Older calls</h3><p>Some earlier predictions did not include a timing-width estimate. We infer one in a fixed, documented way and label it as inferred rather than pretending the model supplied it.</p></article>
        </div>
        <div class="formula-block">
          <span>Why two horizons?</span>
          <strong>Near-term calls cover 6 hours; next-day calls cover 24 hours. Both count 50%.</strong>
          <span>Overall forecast quality</span>
          <strong>100 × [1 − ½(near-term error ÷ 540) − ½(next-day error ÷ 1,620)]</strong>
        </div>
        <p class="method-caption">The numbers set the scale of the two deadlines; they are not a model’s chosen ETA. A model needs at least one scored bet in both timelines before receiving a topline score. The result is bounded from 0 to 100 for display and is not a percent-accuracy claim.</p>
        <div class="worked-example">
          <p class="kicker">Why retain confidence too?</p>
          <p>Confidence answers “does this named outcome happen by the deadline?” Sigma answers “if it happens, how tightly can I locate its time?” CRPS evaluates both in one forecast distribution.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="selection" class="scoring-section">
        <div class="scoring-section__heading"><p class="kicker">06</p><h2>Where did the model look?</h2></div>
        <p>Whole forecast error judges the complete probability forecast. This second test asks a simpler question: did the model spend one of its eight picks on a base where something happened?</p>
        <div class="rule-grid rule-grid--two">
          <article class="rule-card"><h3>Finds captures</h3><p>The share of selected bases that were captured by the deadline. Use it to see whether the model spent its limited picks on places where a capture actually happened, even if another part of its call was wrong.</p></article>
          <article class="rule-card"><h3>Strongest picks</h3><p>Ranks 1 and 5 are the highest-priority short and next-day calls. Their capture rate shows whether the model’s first choices outperform the rest of its list.</p></article>
        </div>
        <div class="formula-block formula-block--compact"><span>Finds action vs. field <span class="technical-label">(capture lift)</span></span><strong>model capture rate ÷ matched eligible-base capture rate</strong></div>
        <p class="method-caption">The baseline is recalculated for every scored call using all strategic bases available at that cutoff and deadline. A lift of 1.00× matches the field; 2.00× finds captures twice as often. This helps compare quiet and chaotic periods without using model confidence.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="censoring" class="scoring-section">
        <div class="scoring-section__heading"><p class="kicker">07</p><h2>When we decline to score</h2></div>
        <p>A call is <b>not scored</b> (technical name: <span class="technical-label">censored</span>) when the observations cannot support a fair result. That is a completed settlement state, but it contributes neither credit nor a miss to the model average.</p>
        <ul class="rationale-list">
          <li><strong>Missing coverage.</strong> A gap greater than two expected polling intervals—currently 30 minutes—means we may have missed or badly localized a transition.</li>
          <li><strong>Ambiguous timing.</strong> Normal polling intervals are used directly. A larger gap cannot localize an event fairly, so we leave the call unscored.</li>
          <li><strong>War boundary.</strong> If a war ends before a call’s ETA-plus-three-hour window closes, and no qualifying transition was already observed, we leave it unscored rather than calling it a miss.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section id="rationale" class="scoring-section">
        <div class="scoring-section__heading"><p class="kicker">08</p><h2>Why score it this way?</h2></div>
        <ol class="rationale-list rationale-list--numbered">
          <li><strong>Make every claim falsifiable.</strong> A named base, event, exact ETA, and confidence is more informative than “something may happen in 24 hours.”</li>
          <li><strong>Force and balance short- and longer-range judgment.</strong> The four-within-6h plus four-from-6–24h split prevents a model from filling a round only with safer, vague next-day bets. Fixed timeline scales keep longer CRPS integration windows from dominating the topline score.</li>
          <li><strong>Match scoring precision to observation precision.</strong> Interval-aware CRPS uses the whole API observation interval rather than inventing an exact event minute.</li>
          <li><strong>Recognize the structure of Foxhole captures.</strong> Destroyed and captured are distinct outcomes, but both correctly anticipate loss of the current owner; the 0.75 rule captures that shared information.</li>
          <li><strong>Close every fair bet.</strong> The three-hour grace window ensures non-events receive a finite probabilistic penalty rather than remaining unresolved indefinitely.</li>
          <li><strong>Avoid subjective grading.</strong> Evidence and relevance ratings remain visible for human review, while numerical settlement depends only on prospective forecasts and official API observations.</li>
        </ol>
      </section>

      <p class="method-version">Current protocol: <code>event_outcome_v5_crps</code>. Existing event-outcome rounds are rescored with inferred sigma; new rounds ask the model for sigma directly. A new war receives a two-hour telemetry warm-up before forecasting begins.</p>
    </main>

    <footer><div class="page-width"><strong>FOXHOLE FORECAST</strong><a href={basePath}>Live dashboard</a></div></footer>
  </body>
</html>
